```java
// T is not "any type" -- it must support compareTo
static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(T a, T b) {
    return a.compareTo(b) >= 0 ? a : b;
}

max(3, 5);           // OK -- Integer implements Comparable<Integer>
max("cat", "dog");    // OK -- String implements Comparable<String>
max(new Object(), new Object());  // compile error -- Object has no compareTo
```

Without the `extends Comparable<T>` bound, `max`'s body couldn't call `a.compareTo(b)` at all — a fully unconstrained `T` gives you *zero* operations beyond what every possible type supports (assignment, reference equality), and comparison isn't one of them. The bound is what makes the method body's `a.compareTo(b)` call legal, by promising the compiler that whatever `T` ends up being, it will have a `compareTo` method.

This is the generic-programming pattern in one line: parameterize over the type, but constrain that type parameter to exactly the capabilities the function's body actually needs — no more, no less.
